<Base title="Music Copyright and Royalty Streams for Songwriters">
<section class="route">
  <div class="wrap section">
    <p class="kicker">Writing</p>
    <h1 data-split>Music Copyright and Royalty Streams for Songwriters</h1>
    <p class="lede prose">Explains the two copyright types behind every song, how music publishers make money, and the five royalty streams songwriters and publishers split.</p>
    <p class="links-line">Originally published in Traditional Chinese &middot; <a href="/writing">All writing</a></p>
    <div class="longform prose" style="margin-top:34px">
      <p class="rv">Music copyright has several revenue streams, and the business models behind songwriting shape both creator rights and creator income. Most songwriters don't fully understand how record labels and publishers turn copyrights into money, and that gap costs them earnings they're owed. In this post, I break down the scope of music copyright, the main business models, how labels generate profit, and how revenue gets split with creators, so you understand your negotiation leverage.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">Introduction</h2>
      <p class="rv">Music copyright is a complex field. Companies focus on different aspects of it depending on their core business, and music publishers build their operations around rights derived from musical compositions. In this post, I cover what music publishers do, the main music copyright revenue streams, and who they serve.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">Introduction to Musical Works</h2>
      <h3 class="rv">What Is a Musical Work?</h3>
      <p class="rv">Every song consists of two separate copyrights: the musical composition and the sound recording.</p>

      <div class="before-after-grid rv" style="margin:20px 0">
        <div class="ba-card" style="border-color:color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 40%, transparent);background:var(--bg-raise)">
          <span class="ba-tag" style="color:var(--accent-ink)"><svg class="pill-i" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="4.25" cy="11.75" r="2.1"/><circle cx="12" cy="9.75" r="2.1"/><path d="M6.35 11.75V3.5L14.1 1.9v7.85"/><path d="M6.35 6.15 14.1 4.55"/></svg>Musical Composition</span>
          <p style="margin:0"><strong>Lyrics &amp; Melody:</strong> Owned by songwriters and lyricists. Administered by music publishers and CMOs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ba-card" style="border-color:color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 40%, transparent);background:var(--bg-raise)">
          <span class="ba-tag" style="color:var(--accent-ink)"><svg class="pill-i" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="5.75" y="1.5" width="4.5" height="8" rx="2.25"/><path d="M3.5 7.75a4.5 4.5 0 0 0 9 0M8 12.25v2.25M5.75 14.5h4.5"/></svg>Sound Recording (Master)</span>
          <p style="margin:0"><strong>Recorded Audio:</strong> Owned by recording artists, producers, and record labels.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="rv">What Can You Do with a Musical Work?</h3>
      <p class="rv">Works recognized under copyright law grant the original creator exclusive rights to adapt, perform, and reproduce their work. Sound recordings and musical compositions both fall under these protections, so creators can monetize their work through publishing.</p>

      <h2 class="rv">Overview of Music Publishers</h2>
      <h3 class="rv">What Does a Music Publisher Do?</h3>
      <p class="rv">Publishers work with songwriters to match commercial opportunities, license musical works, and collect licensing fees. Through licensing agreements, publisher and creator split the revenue, typically 50/50. Songwriters with strong bargaining power and negotiation skills can push that up to 75%, though creators should consult an attorney about contract terms.</p>

      <h3 class="rv">5 Primary Music Publisher Royalty Streams</h3>
      <table class="cheat-sheet rv" style="margin:20px 0">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Stream</th>
            <th>Trigger Event</th>
            <th>Calculation / Rate Base</th>
            <th>Typical Collection Flow</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1. Public Performance</strong></td>
            <td>Broadcast on radio/TV, concerts, venue playback.</td>
            <td>Statutory tariffs per venue/medium.</td>
            <td>CMOs (MÜST, PRS) &rarr; Publisher &rarr; Songwriter</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Synchronization (Sync)</strong></td>
            <td>Music paired with visual media (films, ads, games).</td>
            <td>Negotiated flat fee buyout ($35k&ndash;$400k).</td>
            <td>Client &rarr; Publisher/Label &rarr; Songwriter</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>3. Mechanical Royalties</strong></td>
            <td>Physical CDs, vinyl, or digital audio streaming reproduction.</td>
            <td>PPD &times; statutory rate (UK 8.5%, TW 5.4%).</td>
            <td>Record Label &rarr; Publisher &rarr; Songwriter</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>4. Print Music</strong></td>
            <td>Sheet music publication and orchestral score rental.</td>
            <td>Per-sheet royalty or recurring rental fee.</td>
            <td>Publisher &rarr; Classical Composer</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>5. Grand Rights</strong></td>
            <td>Dramatic musical theater &amp; stage performances.</td>
            <td>Flat fee or % of box office gate.</td>
            <td>Theatrical Production &rarr; Publisher &rarr; Composer</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h4 class="rv">Opportunity Matching, Licensing, and Fee Collection</h4>
      <ul class="now-list" style="max-width:none"><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Opportunity Matching: Publishers bridge the gap between music users (film production companies, advertising agencies, game studios) and songwriters. When a client needs music for a specific context, the publisher pitches suitable tracks from their roster.</span></li><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Licensing: This refers to licensing the musical composition. For example, if a YouTuber wants to cover a song by a signed songwriter, they must obtain a license from the composition copyright holder, which the publisher manages.</span></li><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Collecting Licensing Fees: Following the example above, after obtaining the license, the YouTuber pays a fee to the publisher, which then distributes the creator's share according to their agreement.</span></li></ul>
      <p class="rv">Publishers typically delegate performance licensing and fee collection to collective management organizations (CMOs). The CMO periodically reports song usage and disburses royalties to the publisher, which frees publishers to focus on finding placement opportunities.</p>
      <p class="rv">Related link: <a href="https://musicgoldencircle.com/tipo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Taiwan Copyright Collective Management Organizations (台灣著作權集管協會)</a></p>
      <h3 class="rv">Music Publisher Revenue Streams</h3>
      <p class="rv">Publishers monetize the rights protected under musical compositions, including public performance, synchronization, and mechanical reproduction rights, into royalty revenue. Below are the five primary streams, ordered roughly by typical revenue share.</p>
      <p class="rv">Copyright protections and royalty rates vary by country. Public performance and mechanical royalty rates depend on local regulations, playback mediums, broadcast times, and release formats. While this breakdown focuses on UK copyright standards alongside Taiwan copyright laws, feel free to share your thoughts in the comments.</p>
      <h4 class="rv">1. Public Performance Royalties</h4>
      <p class="rv">Creators earn performance royalties whenever their music plays publicly, whether that's a venue or a broadcast. To play music legally, commercial businesses must obtain licenses from collective management organizations, which then distribute the collected royalties to publishers and independent songwriters.</p>
      <h4 class="rv">2. Synchronization Royalties (Sync)</h4>
      <p class="rv">Sync royalties apply when music is paired with visual media, such as films, video games, or commercials. Some countries use fixed statutory rates, but in the UK and US, sync licensing fees depend entirely on negotiations among the record label, publisher, and music user. Sync licenses are typically granted on a one-time, buyout basis.</p>
      <p class="rv">For example, a film might use 10 seconds of a track in a scene but need 30 seconds for the trailer. Because different use cases need different durations, a one-off fee works better for clients.</p>
      <p class="rv">Typical fees depend on the client's budget and negotiation. For US commercial feature films, main opening themes range from $75,000 to $400,000, while end credits themes range from $35,000 to $250,000.</p>
      <h4 class="rv">3. Mechanical Royalties</h4>
      <p class="rv">A songwriter licenses a user, typically a record label, to record and reproduce a composition onto physical audio formats or audiovisual media. In exchange, the label pays mechanical royalties for the rights to reproduce, distribute, and sell it.</p>
      <p class="rv">Calculating mechanical royalties requires two concepts:</p>
      <ul class="now-list" style="max-width:none"><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Royalty rate: Varies by jurisdiction. The statutory rate in the UK is 8.5% (the same for physical and digital downloads), while in Taiwan it is typically 5.4%.</span></li><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Pricing base: Calculated using either Retail Selling Price (RSP) or Published Price to Dealer (PPD / wholesale price). Both the UK and Taiwan calculate based on PPD. (The US uses a different statutory per-track system.)</span></li></ul>
      <p class="rv">Mechanical Royalty (MR) Formula:</p>
      <p class="rv">PPD × Royalty Rate × Units Sold × (1 / Number of Tracks × 2 [Songwriter &amp; Composer])</p>
      <p class="rv">Example:</p>
      <p class="rv">Suppose a 12-track album has a wholesale price (PPD) of NT$200. The record label ships 1,000 copies to retail stores, and all 1,000 sell out.</p>
      <p class="rv">Using the formula above, one songwriter earns NT$450 in royalties for one track.</p>
      <p class="rv">If the agreed split between the songwriter and publisher is 60/40:</p>
      <ul class="now-list" style="max-width:none"><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Songwriter share: NT$450 × 60% = NT$270</span></li><li><span class="dot"></span><span>Publisher share: NT$450 × 40% = NT$180</span></li></ul>
      <h4 class="rv">4. Print</h4>
      <p class="rv">This covers sheet music publications. Sheet music isn't a primary revenue stream for mainstream pop songwriters, but it's essential for classical composers. Orchestras often rent scores from publishers instead of buying them outright, which gives publishers ongoing rental income.</p>
      <h4 class="rv">5. Grand Rights</h4>
      <p class="rv">Grand rights cover the dramatic performance of musical works in theatrical productions, such as musicals like <em>Mamma Mia!</em> or <em>The Lion King</em>. Licensing music composed for stage plays and musical theater falls under grand rights. Productions typically pay royalties as a flat fee or an agreed percentage of box office receipts. (This specific category is not explicitly defined in Taiwan Intellectual Property Office (智慧財產局, TIPO) guidelines; feel free to share your thoughts in the comments if you have more details.)</p>
      <p class="rv">Summary: independent songwriters can collect royalties by joining a CMO. But for creators without industry connections or business resources, signing with a publisher provides access to placement opportunities and eliminates administrative overhead, so you can focus on writing.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">Conclusion</h2>
      <p class="rv">The music industry spans multiple disciplines: recording, publishing, distribution, marketing, and promotion. This post covered the fundamentals of music publishing. I will continue breaking down other industry roles in future posts.</p>
      <p class="rv">Related reading: <a href="/writing/music-industry-structure-and-careers">A guide to music industry structure and career paths</a></p>
